@TiZ-EX1, I knew someone would eventually mention the 4GB thing, I should have "pre-answered" it in my previous comment.

You can use more than 4GB of RAM in 32-bit Linux (and Windows and OS X, and Solaris, And FreeBSD...), using [Physical Address Extensions](http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Physical_Address_Extension). Some modern distributions even enable it by default, so you don't even need to do anything. If it's not enabled, just use your distribution's package manager to install the package and away you go. There is no point in wasting RAM because of this.

What are the extra architectural features of 64-bit operating systems that regular users (and even developers) will take advantage of? (it's a genuine question; there may be things I am unaware of)

@TiZ-EX1, but that's the thing: the majority of programs will not be any faster in 64-bit than they are on 32-bit because most of the architectural changes are meant for taking advantage of the increased capacity in addressing, which only very specific software takes advantage of with serious gains in performance. Multimedia software (video players, encoders, etc), scientific calculations software, maybe some graphics software will take advantage of it, but the rest is pretty much the same.

You may see a slight increase in speed if they take advantage of the extra registers (which, as you said, depends on the compiler, not the developer), but that is minimal and in my opinion doesn't justify having your software take up much more space in RAM.

